1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,126
The brush finds the curve of a pot, painted with dark bands on a red body.

2
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:16,563
It is handsome, it is well made, and it was made in a village — not in a city with drains and standard bricks,

3
00:00:16,563 --> 00:00:21,123
but in a settlement of a few hundred people who had just started using metal.

4
00:00:28,133 --> 00:00:29,559
Again, only one source.

5
00:00:29,559 --> 00:00:37,941
These people left no writing, so once more everything comes out of the ground: their pots, their copper, their houses and their graves.

6
00:00:44,133 --> 00:00:46,853
The word is Chalcolithic — copper-stone.

7
00:00:52,733 --> 00:01:00,871
It names a stage, not a people: the time when communities used copper but had not yet worked out how to make bronze or iron in quantity,

8
00:01:00,871 --> 00:01:04,138
and so went on using stone tools alongside metal ones.

9
00:01:04,138 --> 00:01:05,444
Copper for some jobs.

10
00:01:05,444 --> 00:01:06,932
Stone for the rest.

11
00:01:13,733 --> 00:01:20,870
These were many separate cultures, not one, and archaeologists name them after the site where each was first identified.

12
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:30,458
Ahar, in southern Rajasthan, where people worked copper close to its source.

13
00:01:30,458 --> 00:01:33,928
Kayatha and Malwa, in the Narmada and Chambal country.

14
00:01:33,928 --> 00:01:38,013
And Jorwe, across much of Maharashtra, the largest of them.

15
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:46,781
What did a Chalcolithic village look like?

16
00:01:46,781 --> 00:01:49,996
Houses of mud and thatch, round or rectangular.

17
00:01:49,996 --> 00:01:52,274
Storage jars sunk into the floor.

18
00:01:52,274 --> 00:01:54,919
Wheel-made pottery, often painted.

19
00:01:58,867 --> 00:02:03,862
Fields of wheat, barley, rice, lentils and millets, depending on where you stood.

20
00:02:03,862 --> 00:02:05,385
Cattle, sheep and goats.

21
00:02:05,385 --> 00:02:10,864
These were farming villages, confident and well organised, and they were not cities.

22
00:02:16,333 --> 00:02:18,160
Two sites are worth knowing.

23
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:25,387
At Inamgaon in Maharashtra, people built a channel and an embankment to manage water — careful, planned work.

24
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:35,061
And at Daimabad, also in Maharashtra, somebody found four heavy bronze objects:

25
00:02:35,061 --> 00:02:39,071
a chariot drawn by oxen, a buffalo, an elephant and a rhinoceros.

26
00:02:39,071 --> 00:02:45,568
They are the finest metal casting from this world, and they are unlike anything else found with them.

27
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:51,862
And that is exactly the problem.

28
00:02:51,862 --> 00:02:58,257
The Daimabad bronzes were not found in a controlled excavation, so there is no clean layer to date them by.

29
00:03:06,933 --> 00:03:10,584
Some scholars place them with the late Harappan phase at the site.

30
00:03:10,584 --> 00:03:13,253
Others argue the casting points to a later date.

31
00:03:13,253 --> 00:03:18,348
Without the layer, the argument cannot be settled, and both readings stay on the table.

32
00:03:22,267 --> 00:03:25,429
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

33
00:03:25,429 --> 00:03:26,817
Copper and stone.

34
00:03:26,817 --> 00:03:28,582
Village, not city.

35
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:36,249
Dig Quiz.

36
00:03:36,249 --> 00:03:37,610
Pause and name it.

37
00:03:37,610 --> 00:03:42,980
First: a copper axe found beside a chipped stone blade, in the same house.

38
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:46,216
That is the Chalcolithic, by definition.

39
00:03:51,967 --> 00:03:56,450
Second: a wheel-made pot, dark bands painted on a red surface.

40
00:03:56,450 --> 00:04:00,450
Black-on-red ware, the signature of these cultures.

41
00:04:04,533 --> 00:04:10,168
Third: a village in Maharashtra with a channel and an embankment to manage water.

42
00:04:10,168 --> 00:04:10,855
Inamgaon.

43
00:04:10,855 --> 00:04:16,378
In Mains, a useful question asks why Chalcolithic India produced no cities.

44
00:04:22,033 --> 00:04:27,905
Point to scale, to rainfall and soil, and to the absence of the surplus and the trade that city life needs.

45
00:04:34,833 --> 00:04:41,500
A painted pot takes its place in the gallery: the everyday object of a world that farmed well and never built a city.

46
00:04:46,667 --> 00:04:50,807
Next gallery: the cities that did get built, on the Indus.

47
00:04:50,807 --> 00:04:55,107
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

